const EN = `# Getting Around in Vietnam

Vietnam is easy to move around once you know the main options. In big cities, most visitors use ride-hailing apps, taxis, and walking. Between cities, you can fly, take a train, or take a bus. Prices, routes, and times can change, so check the latest details before you go.

---

## Arriving at the Airport
- **Hanoi:** Noi Bai Airport (HAN) is about 30 km north of the city center. The trip to the Old Quarter usually takes 30 to 60 minutes, depending on traffic.
- **Ho Chi Minh City:** Tan Son Nhat Airport (SGN) is only about 7 km from District 1. Traffic can still make the trip slow.
- Good ways to reach the city are ride-hailing apps, official airport taxis, and the airport buses.
- In Hanoi, Express Bus 86 goes from the airport to the center and is a popular budget choice. In Ho Chi Minh City, Bus 109 goes to the District 1 area.
- Only use the official taxi desks or taxi queues. Do not follow people who offer you a ride inside the arrivals hall.
- Allow extra time when you go back to the airport, even for a domestic flight.

---

## Ride-Hailing Apps
- Grab, Xanh SM, and Be are the main ride-hailing apps. Grab is often the easiest for visitors because the app is in English and works with foreign cards. Some local apps may not accept foreign cards, so check before you ride.
- You see the price in the app before the ride starts. This helps you avoid arguments about the fare.
- You can choose a car or a motorbike. A car is better with luggage or in the rain.
- You need mobile data to use the apps. A local SIM card or an eSIM is very helpful.
- Check the car and the license plate before you get in.
- At busy places, such as airports, the pickup point can be hard to find. Read the pickup instructions in the app.

---

## Taxis
- Taxis are easy to find in Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City.
- Choose well-known companies, such as Mai Linh, Vinasun, or Xanh SM, and ask the driver to use the meter.
- Do not accept a fixed price from a driver on the street. It is often higher than the meter.
- Show your hotel name or address on your phone. Writing it in Vietnamese helps.
- If the meter looks wrong or the driver refuses to use it, leave the taxi and find another one.
- Small extra fees, such as airport or toll fees, can be normal. Ask the driver if you are not sure.

---

## City Buses
- City buses are very cheap and are used by many local people.
- They can be slow and crowded, and signs are not always in English.
- Use a map app to find the stop and the bus number.
- Keep small cash with you. Ask the staff on the bus how to pay.
- Buses are best for short trips when you do not have much luggage.

---

## Metro
- **Hanoi:** Two elevated metro lines work in the western part of the city, Line 2A and Line 3. They are useful for some areas, but they do not go through the Old Quarter yet.
- **Ho Chi Minh City:** Metro Line 1 opened in December 2024. It runs from Ben Thanh in District 1 to Suoi Tien, with 14 stations.
- In Ho Chi Minh City, Line 1 is useful for places such as Ben Thanh Market, the Opera House, and Thao Dien. It does not go to the airport yet.
- Metro hours and ticket rules can change. Check the official information at the station or on the operator's website.

---

## Motorbike Taxis
- Motorbike taxis are fast in heavy traffic. You can book one in a ride-hailing app.
- Always wear the helmet. Hold on and keep your bag in front of you.
- If you take a motorbike taxi from the street, agree on the price before you start.
- Do not choose a motorbike if you have big luggage, if it rains hard, or if you feel unsure.

---

## Renting a Motorbike or a Car
- Many travelers rent a motorbike, but traffic in Vietnam is busy and can be dangerous for new riders.
- To ride legally, you usually need your home driving license with the right motorbike category, and an International Driving Permit (IDP) that follows the 1968 Vienna Convention.
- Not all IDPs follow this rule. An IDP from the older 1949 Geneva Convention may not be valid in Vietnam. Check which type you have before you travel.
- Many rental shops do not ask for a license. But if you have an accident or a police check, you can have problems with the police and with your insurance.
- Check your travel insurance. Many policies do not cover riders without a valid license.
- Never ride or drive after drinking alcohol. Vietnam has a zero limit for drivers.
- If you are not sure, use ride-hailing apps, or hire a car with a driver instead.

---

## Trains
- The main train line goes from Hanoi to Ho Chi Minh City. It is about 1,700 km long, and the whole trip takes more than one day.
- Many travelers take only part of the route. Popular stops include Ninh Binh, Hue, Da Nang, and Nha Trang.
- A night train goes from Hanoi to Lao Cai. This is a common way to travel to Sapa.
- Trains have seats and sleeper beds. For night trains, a sleeper bed is more comfortable.
- You can buy tickets at the station or online. The official Vietnam Railways website is dsvn.vn. Some foreign cards do not work well on this website, so you may need to try another card or another way to pay.
- Book early for public holidays and busy travel times, especially around Tet.

---

## Domestic Flights
- Flying is the fastest way to travel between far cities. Hanoi to Ho Chi Minh City takes about two hours.
- Vietnam Airlines and Vietjet Air are two well-known airlines for domestic flights.
- Check the baggage rules before you book. Low-cost flights often have strict limits.
- Arrive at the airport early. Flights can be delayed, especially in bad weather.

---

## Buses Between Cities
- Long-distance buses connect many cities and towns. Sleeper buses and small "limousine" vans are common choices.
- Choose a company with good reviews. Ask where the bus leaves from and where it stops.
- Keep your passport, phone, and money with you, not in your luggage.
- Roads can be slow in the mountains and in bad weather. Do not plan a tight schedule on the same day.

---

## Crossing the Road
- Traffic in Vietnam is full of motorbikes, and it may feel scary at first.
- Do not run. Wait for a gap, then walk at a steady, slow speed. Drivers will move around you.
- Do not stop suddenly or step back in the middle of the road.
- Use a pedestrian crossing or a traffic light when you can. Cross with local people when you are not sure.

---

## Safety and Scam Tips
- Use apps or official taxis, and check the price before you ride.
- Keep your phone and bag safe. Do not hold your phone near the road edge, because someone on a motorbike can grab it.
- Do not give your passport to a stranger. A hotel or a rental shop may ask to copy it, but keep the original with you if you can.
- If a driver asks for a very high price, say no and find another ride.
- Save your hotel address and emergency numbers on your phone.

---

## Simple Planning Tips
- Download your ride-hailing app and a map app before you arrive.
- Get a local SIM card or an eSIM at the airport or before your trip.
- Keep some small cash for buses, markets, and small shops.
- Do not plan too many cities in a few days. Distances in Vietnam are long.
- Leave extra time for airports, traffic, and busy holidays.`

